        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe individual service methods (`isCityOverSoftCapacity`, `isEmailDomainDisposableAdjacent`) and the base-label derivation are well-placed and idiomatic. The problem is the aggregation logic.\n\n**The three-signal risk rule is duplicated verbatim across the two controllers.**\n\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.computeRiskFlag` (lines 191-195) and the inlined lambda in `OwnerRestControllerV2.listOwnersPage` (lines 44-46) contain the exact same expression:\n  `Boolean.TRUE.equals(getPossibleDuplicate()) || isEmailDomainDisposableAdjacent(email) || isCityOverSoftCapacity(city)`.\n- V1 gave this rule a name and a Javadoc precisely because it's a non-trivial business rule (it encodes the spec). Copy-pasting that same named rule as an anonymous inline expression in V2 means the spec now lives in two authoritative places. Adding a fourth signal, or changing one, requires editing both in lockstep with no compiler help if they drift.\n\n**Suggested fix:** give the aggregation a single home. The most idiomatic option here is to push it into the service layer, since two of the three inputs are already `ClinicService` predicates \u2014 e.g. a `boolean isOwnerRiskFlagged(possibleDuplicate, email, city)` (or an overload taking the relevant fields) that both controllers call. That keeps the spec's OR-composition in one place and leaves the controllers doing only `setRiskFlag(...)`.\n\nNote: this is the same pattern already used for `bulkSignupWarning`/`capacityWarning`, but those are trivial single-call setters; the risk rule is a genuine multi-part rule and warrants deduplication rather than following the inline precedent. If deduplication is deliberately declined, at minimum V2 should call a shared helper rather than re-inlining the composed expression."
